      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data for a new low-fat mayo formulation and hitting some stability issues in our accelerated aging tests. The pH is quite low (around 3.5) and we're seeing significant phase separation after 48 hours. Can you walk me through the specific role lecithin plays in stabilizing the oil-water interface in these high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a natural emulsifier derived from soybeans, acts as a surfactant that lowers the interfacial tension between water and oil. In low pH conditions, its amphiphilic nature helps stabilize the oil-water interface. Lecithin molecules arrange themselves with hydrophilic heads towards water and hydrophobic tails towards oil, thus preventing phase separation. Its thioester content also stabilizes it against hydrolysis, making it effective in acidic environments. This stabilization occurs even at a pH of 3.5, preserving the integrity of the mayo formulation.",
