    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm specifically struggling with the kinetic stability. Even with the lecithin, it feels like the droplets are being forced out of the emulsion by the acidity. Is there a way to describe the interaction between the proton concentration and the surfactant's ability to maintain that interface? I need to understand if the acid is actively disrupting the layer or just making it less efficient.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Acidic conditions can indeed impact the efficacy of surfactants like lecithin. High proton concentrations can protonate the surfactant's head groups, reducing their charge and thus their ability to reduce interfacial tension effectively. This protonation can decrease the electrostatic repulsion between the heads, which in turn weakens the stability of the emulsion. As the lecithin becomes less efficient in its role as an emulsifier under these conditions, the richer acid propels the oil droplets out of the water, leading to phase separation.\n\n\nTo minimize this effect, you may consider adjusting the formulation by incorporating additional stabilizers that are less susceptible to acid degradation or by using modified lecithins with stronger acid resistance. Such additives can counter the impact of increased proton concentration, retaining the oil droplets within the aqueous phase and preserving the stability of your mayo product. It's also crucial to validate these changes with rigorous pH stability testing to ensure that the reformulation addresses the kinetic stability issues without compromising the desired low-fat profile.",
      "source": "target",
